❯ how can I underline numbers using the print function in python                
  ⎿  1 skill available

⏺ Python's print has no underline option of its own. Most terminals, including
  macOS Terminal and the VS Code terminal, understand ANSI escape codes: special
  character sequences that switch text styles on and off.

  x = 0.5
  print(f"P_3({x}) = \033[4m{-0.4375}\033[0m")

  - \033[4m turns underlining on. \033 is the escape character, and 4 is the
    code for underline.
  - \033[0m resets the style back to normal.

  The reset matters. If you leave off \033[0m, everything printed afterward
  stays underlined, including your shell prompt once the script ends.

  A few related codes use the same pattern: 1 for bold, 31 for red text, 32 for
  green text. You can combine them with a semicolon, e.g. \033[1;4m for bold and
  underlined.

  Caveats:
  - These codes only display as styling in a terminal. If you redirect the
    output to a file (python legendre.py > out.txt), the file will contain the
    raw \033[4m characters.
  - Some editors' built-in output panes don't interpret them and show junk
    characters instead.

  Quick check: before running it, predict what this prints:

